The  command attaches the AmigaDOS filesystem residing on the de‐
vice to the global filesystem namespace at the location indicated
by Both and are converted to absolute  paths  before  use.   This
command  is normally executed by at boot time, but can be used by
any user to mount an AmigaDOS file system on any  directory  that
they  own (provided, of course, that they have appropriate access
to the device that contains the file system).  The options are as
follows: Use the specified mount as described in Set the owner of
the files in the file system to The default owner is the owner of
the directory on which the file system is being mounted.  Set the
group of the files in the file system to The default group is the
group of the directory on which the file system is being mounted.
Specify the maximum file permissions for files in the  file  sys‐
tem.   (For  example,  a  mask of specifies that, by default, the
owner should have read, write, and execute permissions for files,
but others should only have read and  execute  permissions.   See
for more information about octal file modes.)  Only the nine low‐
order  bits  of are used.  The default mask is taken from the di‐
rectory on which the file system is being mounted.   The  utility
first  appeared  in  The  filesystem currently supports the Amiga
fast file system.  The  filesystem  implementation  currently  is
read‐only.   The utility silently retries the mount read‐only, as
if the option were specified, when it encounters the error.
